{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 8,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.049,
            0.432,
            0.066
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 7 | Part-II"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.088,
            0.537,
            0.108
          ],
          "content": "Subtracting 7 from both sides we get"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.351,
            0.108,
            0.647,
            0.127
          ],
          "content": "2y + 7 – 7 = 21 – 7, which gives"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.459,
            0.133,
            0.539,
            0.15
          ],
          "content": "2 y = 1 4 .",
          "image_source": "images/page_8_equation_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.156,
            0.715,
            0.176
          ],
          "content": "We can directly find the value of y, seeing that $2 \\times ( 7 ) = 1 4$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.18,
            0.577,
            0.2
          ],
          "content": "We can also divide both sides by 2 to find y."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.366,
            0.204,
            0.632,
            0.225
          ],
          "content": "2y ÷ 2 = 14 ÷ 2, which gives"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.468,
            0.229,
            0.527,
            0.249
          ],
          "content": "y = 7 .",
          "image_source": "images/page_8_equation_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
            0.265,
            0.225,
            0.302
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_8_image_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.227,
            0.276,
            0.647,
            0.297
          ],
          "content": "Remember, it is always good to check your solution."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.327,
            0.316,
            0.353
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Figure it Out"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.361,
            0.639,
            0.381
          ],
          "content": "1. Solve these equations and check the solutions."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.187,
            0.386,
            0.352,
            0.405
          ],
          "content": "(a) $3x - 10 = 35$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.188,
            0.411,
            0.306,
            0.43
          ],
          "content": "(b) $5 s = 3 s$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.187,
            0.436,
            0.376,
            0.455
          ],
          "content": "(c) $3u - 7 = 2u + 3$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.188,
            0.46,
            0.439,
            0.48
          ],
          "content": "(d) $4\\left (m+6  \\right ) -8=2m-4$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.188,
            0.491,
            0.299,
            0.523
          ],
          "content": "(e) ${ \\frac { u } { 1 5 } } = 6$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.532,
            0.58,
            0.552
          ],
          "content": "2. Frame an equation that has no solution."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.818,
            0.507,
            0.904,
            0.559
          ],
          "content": "Math Talk"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.563,
            0.801,
            0.601
          ],
          "content": "[**Hint:** 4 more than a number, and 5 more than a number can never be equal!]"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.601,
            0.846,
            0.657
          ],
          "content": "The procedure to systematically solve an equation can be made efficient if we make an observation. Consider the equation $11y+(-5)=61$ which we solved."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.658,
            0.845,
            0.678
          ],
          "content": "As the first step, we subtracted – 5 from both sides to remove the term –5."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.362,
            0.682,
            0.634,
            0.702
          ],
          "content": "11\\bar{y}+(-5)-(-5)=61-(-5).",
          "image_source": "images/page_8_equation_22.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.707,
            0.846,
            0.745
          ],
          "content": "Since this action removes the term – 5 from the LHS, we could have written this step as:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.426,
            0.745,
            0.571,
            0.764
          ],
          "content": "1 1 y = 6 1 - ( - 5 ) .",
          "image_source": "images/page_8_equation_24.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.769,
            0.846,
            0.807
          ],
          "content": "Note that we could also have arrived at this step by seeing addition and subtraction as inverse operations, as in the case of Example 1."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.807,
            0.847,
            0.845
          ],
          "content": "Similarly, when we had 11y = 66, we divided both sides by 11 to remove the factor 11 from the LHS."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.409,
            0.851,
            0.589,
            0.871
          ],
          "content": "11y \\div 11 = 66 \\div 11.",
          "image_source": "images/page_8_equation_27.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.156,
            0.933,
            0.194,
            0.95
          ],
          "content": "172"
        }
      ]
    }
